<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="myo-inositol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2301" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Inositol is the generic name for 1,2,3,4,5,6-cyclohexanehexol. Epimerization of the six hydroxyl groups results in nine stereoisomers. |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL &quot;&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-Inositol&quot;| is the physiologically most common tereoisomer and is abundantly (but not universally) distributed throughout much of the biological system. |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL &quot;&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-Inositol&quot;| is an essential polyol in eukaryotes, where its phospholipid derivative |FRAME: L-1-phosphatidyl-inositols phosphatidylinositol| (PI) is an important constituent of phospholipid membranes. PI is also cleaved by phospholipase C to generate inositol phosphate (IP) and diacyglycerol, both of which are central cell signalling molecules. In prokaryotes inositol is not as widely spread as in eukaryotes. While it is commonly found in archaea, where its derivatives serve an important role in osmotic protection at high temperatures |CITS: [12220406]|, it is absent (or present in very low amounts) from most eubacteria, with the exception of the Actinomycetales group, which includes the streptomycetes, corynebacteria and mycobacteria. In these organisms a phosphorylated inositol is an intermediate in the synthesis of a major thiol called |FRAME: CPD1G-2| |CITS: [11544359]|. Mycothiol is important in maintaining the redox balance in the cell, in protection from oxidative stress, and in cysteine storage. In addition, the corynebacteria and mycobacteria produce several cell wall lipoglycans from PI. In these compounds the lipid moieties which are attached to the PI act as anchors in the cell envelope. PI-containing molecules have been shown to be essential for growth of the fast-growing species |FRAME: TAX-1772|, as mutants lacking PI synthase are not viable |CITS: [10889206]|. In most other eubacteria inositol compounds (small soluble molecules or lipids) are undetected or represent very minor components. Earlier work found that various strains of |FRAME:TAX-562|, |FRAME:TAX-90371| and |FRAME:TAX-286| sp. did not contain any PI |CITS: [4552305][844323]|. Later work discovered readily measurable amounts of PI and PI synthase in |FRAME:TAX-317|, |FRAME:TAX-549|, and Ice&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; strains of |FRAME:TAX-562| (these are strains that promote ice-nucleation) |CITS: [1848220]|. However, phosphatidylinositol in these |FRAME:TAX-562| strains represents less than 0.01% of total phospholipid in the cells |CITS: [1848220]|. In all &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol-producing organisms studied to date &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol is produced via dephosphorylation of L-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol 1-phosphate, which is generated from glucose 6-phosphate in a rate-limiting step by L-&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol 1-phosphate synthase (MIPS).</Description>
